p.src:
Classical mechanics for a point at the packet center would require $\frac{d}{dt}\expect{P}
\approx
F\!\left(\expect{X}\right).$ Quantum mechanics gives instead $\frac{d}{dt}\expect{P}
=
\expect{F(X)}.$ In general, $\expect{F(X)}
\neq
F\!\left(\expect{X}\right).$

